        "math_academy_id": "q-331213",
        "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=2x-y+3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $\u0394x=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\u0394y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |",
        "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $\u0394x$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\n\u0394y=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{\u2032}+2{y}_{1}^{\u2032}+2{y}_{2}^{\u2032}+{y}_{3}^{\u2032})\\cdot \u0394x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{\u2032}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}\u0394x,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{\u2032}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}\u0394x,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{\u2032}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+\u0394x,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{\u2032}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =2x-y+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\u0394y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}\u0394x,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{\u2032} & =2{x}_{1}-{y}_{1}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\u0394y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}\u0394x,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & =2{x}_{2}-{y}_{2}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\u0394y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+\u0394x,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\cdot \u0394x) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{17}{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{\u2032} & =2{x}_{3}-{y}_{3}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-\\frac{17}{6}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\u0394y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $\u0394y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394y & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{\u2032}+2{y}_{1}^{\u2032}+2{y}_{2}^{\u2032}+{y}_{3}^{\u2032})\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{8}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{13}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{16}{6}+\\frac{28}{6}+\\frac{30}{6}+\\frac{13}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{87}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\u0394y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ | $\\frac{29}{12}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\u0394x$ to $x$ and $\u0394y$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+\u0394x \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\u0394y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{29}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\u0394y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ | $\\frac{29}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\,\\frac{11}{4}\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(1)\u2248\\frac{11}{4}.$",
